1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,617
One pass. Two fixed views.

2
00:00:03,050 --> 00:00:07,006
Here’s the eight-camera workflow we’re designing for match day.

3
00:00:08,350 --> 00:00:10,488
Keep the original four cameras.

4
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:14,979
Mount one more outside each corner flag, facing into the pitch.

5
00:00:15,150 --> 00:00:17,483
All eight stay fixed throughout the match.

6
00:00:18,020 --> 00:00:20,331
Walk a ball along both touchlines,

7
00:00:20,331 --> 00:00:23,553
through both penalty areas and into the corners.

8
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:25,780
Check the coverage map.

9
00:00:26,550 --> 00:00:30,520
For a 3D position, two cameras need to see the same target

10
00:00:30,520 --> 00:00:31,929
from different angles.

11
00:00:33,380 --> 00:00:37,244
Use a common trigger and record a flash to check the timing.

12
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:39,840
Calibrate each lens,

13
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:42,651
then match the pitch markings to points you’ve measured.

14
00:00:43,650 --> 00:00:47,446
A pole of known height gives you a reference above the grass.

15
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:52,437
Frames can reach the pitch-side Mac out of order.

16
00:00:53,280 --> 00:00:56,705
PitchTrace Edge groups them by exposure timestamp.

17
00:00:57,550 --> 00:01:00,262
Each group contains views of one instant.

18
00:01:02,050 --> 00:01:06,478
The same sequence of groups feeds all three analyses in parallel.

19
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:11,678
The first analysis maps everyone on the field.

20
00:01:12,120 --> 00:01:14,630
For players, it identifies the team

21
00:01:14,630 --> 00:01:17,091
and reads the shirt number when visible.

22
00:01:17,750 --> 00:01:21,682
Both number nines keep separate tracks, one for each team.

23
00:01:23,350 --> 00:01:27,147
The second analysis recognizes actions across frames.

24
00:01:27,300 --> 00:01:31,163
Here, the standing foot plants and the kicking leg swings through.

25
00:01:31,320 --> 00:01:32,541
That’s a shot.

26
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:36,295
The third analysis tracks the ball in 3D.

27
00:01:37,150 --> 00:01:39,295
A defender blocks one camera.

28
00:01:40,360 --> 00:01:42,629
Two others still see the ball leave the foot

29
00:01:42,629 --> 00:01:44,887
and trace its flight to the ground.

30
00:01:46,350 --> 00:01:49,674
Select the match and confirm all eight recordings.

31
00:01:49,980 --> 00:01:52,311
At the whistle, start the match clock.

32
00:01:52,800 --> 00:01:56,940
Edge combines positions, actions and ball flight by match time

33
00:01:56,940 --> 00:01:59,582
and sends the scene to PitchTrace Live.

34
00:02:00,380 --> 00:02:02,285
You can try the viewer today.

35
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:07,335
Start the local gateway, open Live, and choose Local sample.

36
00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:09,909
Freeze the play as the pass arrives.

37
00:02:11,550 --> 00:02:14,194
Move the virtual camera to see who’s free,

38
00:02:14,194 --> 00:02:16,759
then select Resume live to catch up.

39
00:02:19,350 --> 00:02:22,726
Here’s a goal from the built-in match. Nine scores.

40
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:26,779
Wind back to the tackle and look across the pitch.

41
00:02:27,470 --> 00:02:30,658
Seven has drawn the defender in. Watch nine.

42
00:02:32,170 --> 00:02:34,692
He’s moving before the ball comes loose.

43
00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:39,784
Play it at half speed and follow his run into the finish.

44
00:02:41,380 --> 00:02:43,647
Remember how we lined up the frames?

45
00:02:44,320 --> 00:02:48,795
Open the two-camera test archive and pause at twenty-five seconds.

46
00:02:49,150 --> 00:02:51,528
Switch from camera A to camera B.

47
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:55,097
The view changes. The match clock stays still.

48
00:02:55,800 --> 00:02:58,576
Try the 3D viewer through the link below.
